 \begin{abstract}
-Local ancestry inference (LAI) assigns genomic segments to ancestral source populations. Genealogies can inform these assignments by showing how a query genome is related to reference genomes, but recombination changes those relationships along a chromosome. The challenge is to combine evidence from complete local genealogies while preserving sampled haplotype identity as the trees change. We introduce TreeLoom, a neural model that processes every node of an inferred local tree and follows the same sampled haplotypes through the ordered trees. Messages moving toward and away from the root combine evidence across each genealogy. Structural features describe changes between neighboring trees, sample memory carries the history of each sampled haplotype, and a final recurrent network combines genomic context before ancestry prediction. On eight reused European development simulations, TreeLoom reaches $97.08\pm0.57\%$ span accuracy for modern queries, compared with $94.45\pm1.15\%$ for the evaluated ARGMix release and $93.78\pm0.84\%$ for a derived four source AncestralPaths endpoint. These are comparisons of fitted pipelines with different inputs and training, rather than exact publication reproductions. A matched control without added sample memory reaches $97.00\pm0.50\%$ and has higher boundary F1. The results motivate learning from complete genealogies while showing that accurate genomic assignment and accurate tract boundaries remain distinct objectives.
